Yes. Surface moves can change while the underlying operators stay the same: incentives, dependencies, bottlenecks, status games, information asymmetries, feedback loops, and who can impose costs on whom.

The useful discipline is to separate three layers: what is visibly happening, what mechanism would generate it, and what evidence would distinguish that mechanism from a simpler explanation. That keeps you from being distracted by surface shuffling without slipping into assuming hidden coordination where there may only be shared incentives or emergent behavior.

So the move is not merely “look deeper.” It is: track persistent structure across changing appearances, then test whether the structure actually predicts the next move. If it does, keep it. If not, revise it.